-*- coding:utf-8 -*-

build-tool-paths-unquoted — the build breaks when a tool lives in a path with spaces
=====================================================================================

Reported from: the SCHMS+ CI on a Windows runner (fabrik, project
dmoe-pop/epure/es/schms), building clautolisp from source in-job.
2026-08-06.

Symptom
-------

    $ make build-programs
    /usr/bin/sh: line 1: C:/Program: No such file or directory
    make: *** [Makefile:194: build-programs] Error 127

`sh` split the command on the space in `C:/Program Files/Steel Bank
Common Lisp/sbcl` and went looking for an executable literally named
`C:/Program`. Exit 127 is "command not found", not a build error.

The machine is a stock Windows developer workstation. Its tools are
where Windows installers put them:

    C:\Program Files\Steel Bank Common Lisp
    C:\Program Files\Git\bin
    C:\Program Files\dotnet
    C:\Program Files\Microsoft Visual Studio\18\Community\MSBuild\Current\Bin
    C:\Program Files\make-4.4.1-with-guile-w32\bin

Every one of those contains a space. On Windows this is the normal case,
not an exotic one.

Cause
-----

Recipes interpolate the tool variable without quoting it:

    autolisp-front-end/Makefile:240
        $(SBCL) --noinform --no-userinit --non-interactive \

    autolisp-front-end/Makefile:253
        $(CCL) --no-init --quiet \

When `SBCL` resolves to a bare `sbcl` found on PATH — the POSIX case —
this works. When it resolves to an absolute path containing a space, the
shell word-splits it.

Note that the *diagnostics* around those lines already quote correctly:

    autolisp-front-end/Makefile:157
        echo "Error: sbcl not found in PATH (looked for '$(SBCL)')"; \

so the fix is to extend to the invocations the care already taken in the
messages.

Extent
------

Recipe lines interpolating a tool variable unquoted, per Makefile:

    autolisp-front-end/Makefile   4
    clautolisp/Makefile           4
    autolisp-benchmark/Makefile   1
    autolisp-spec/Makefile        1
    autolisp-test/Makefile        1
    documentation/Makefile        1

(counted on `^\t.*[^"]\$\((SBCL|CCL|LISP|CLAUTOLISP|ALFE|PYTHON|EMACS|PANDOC)\)`;
treat it as a lower bound, and as a starting point rather than a
specification — the same reasoning applies to any variable that can hold
a path.)

Three shell scripts interpolate the same variables and want the same
review:

    scripts/run-pathname-probe.sh
    scripts/run-encoding-experiment.sh
    scripts/verify-bricscad-automation.sh

Also worth checking, because the failure mode is identical and silent
until a Windows machine runs it: `$(MAKE) -C <dir>` where the directory
could contain a space, `command -v $(SBCL)` in the HAVE_* probes
(Makefile:58-59 — `command -v` on an unquoted path with spaces answers
about the wrong thing), and any `PREFIX=` / `DESTDIR=` passed down.

Requested
---------

Quote tool variables at their point of use in recipes and scripts:
`"$(SBCL)"`, `"$(CCL)"`, `"$(CLAUTOLISP)"`, `"$(ALFE)"`. Same in the
`command -v` probes, which currently answer the wrong question when the
path has a space.

This is worth doing rather than working around. The workaround on the
caller's side is to set `SBCL=sbcl` and rely on PATH resolution, which
works — the CI now puts the right directories on PATH — but it hides a
defect that will hit the next Windows machine, and it constrains how
callers may configure the build.

Suggested test
--------------

A build with a tool path containing a space, which needs no Windows:

    mkdir -p "/tmp/faux outils"
    ln -s "$(command -v sbcl)" "/tmp/faux outils/sbcl"
    make build-programs SBCL="/tmp/faux outils/sbcl"

That reproduces the failure on Linux and guards the fix afterwards.

Why it matters
--------------

It is what currently stops `build:clautolisp:windows` from succeeding in
the SCHMS+ pipeline, and with it the whole Windows test lane — the CAD
tests included, since they need alfe on the machine that has the CAD.

The Linux lane is unaffected: `sbcl` is found on PATH there, without a
space, so the defect stays invisible until a build runs on Windows.
